WS73 allocated-U RAM loading experiment
======================================

This is an explicit development trial, not an automatically enabled production
board profile. The test module registers only inside the specially tagged VM,
requires an exact serial and observed guest device number, accepts only an
explicit PID 1 bind on the first guest USB port with a boot interface, and
has no module-autoload USB alias or dynamic ID support. A single atomic
admission is consumed before bind, including when bind subsequently fails. Never
install or load it on the host. The normal physical driver remains an observer.
The test does not enable BLE, Wi-Fi or RF functions, change eFuse/flash, reset
a hub/controller or provide a general register interface.

Build from a clean committed tree with four allocated jobs:

  python3 tools/testing/ws73/hardware/build.py

The fixed build-hardware and build-hardware-module directories are protected
by the project's build.lock. The trial module is external to the kernel tree;
its expected out-of-tree taint is recorded and is not a host-module operation.
Kernel/module source, config, symbols and artifacts are archived together.

The explicitly selected development inputs are SDK110's USB reference INI
XO coarse/fine 8/60 (wire 0x3c08), temperature compensation off, and the fixed
calibration-RAM sequence already audited against SDK and libws73 reference
traffic. Original us/ws73.bin, us/wifi_cali.bin and us/btc_cali.bin remain
separate and must all pass production validation before any RAM/XO writes.
Only the U files are supplied: a non-U identity cannot fall back to another
set. Board marketing identity/INI confirmation, production RF calibration,
and cross-board applicability remain unqualified; this bounded experiment
must not install those values as universal driver defaults.

Before attachment, the tagged guest runs eight pure admission cases in a
separate test module and verifies that loading the trial without a device
number fails. The actual trial driver must be absent at READY.

After the normal driver reports exact U READM and the previously observed
ROM version, guest PID 1 checks serial, USB ID, the device number captured
from enumeration and existing observer ownership. Only then does it load
the trial module with that number, recheck ownership, unbind that guest
interface and bind the explicit RAM trial. Automatic worker-context probes,
changed enumeration numbers and repeated probes are rejected. The production loader
performs a fresh HCC identity query before choosing/requesting firmware.
Successful download only means WAIT_RUNTIME. Re-enumeration must create a
new normal observer object and independently report runtime identity/version.
No old identity, URB or callback is transferred. No automatic failure retry
or firmware/profile switch is allowed. Archive every attempt and report
uncaptured wire payload bytes, unexpected enumeration or terminal failure.

No autoload alias alone cannot prevent a loaded USB driver from matching
new devices. The late registration, PID 1 constraint, enumeration binding
and one-shot admission are separate protections; the pure tests do not
qualify real USB matching, firmware loading or RF behavior.

Native HCI experiment
---------------------

The separate `build.py --hci` output enables the existing btws73 auxiliary
consumer and native Bluetooth LE stack, explicitly including CONFIG_BT_LE
and excluding CONFIG_BT_BREDR. It leaves the previous non-HCI build
and its evidence pointer intact. The BLE trial's default behavior still ends
after acknowledged disable. Explicit `native_hci=1` instead publishes the BLE
child after that disable; native open then owns the next enable. Publication
is not an HCI initialization result. Builds without BT_WS73 reject this mode.

Use this option only in the tagged VM's continuous fresh-load experiment:
identity, three-file download, new runtime owner, reference customization,
enable/BOOT and acknowledged disable must precede publication. No HCI traffic
has been sent in that epoch. Do not apply it to an arbitrary retained-runtime
instance. The guest has no BlueZ daemon, network interface or radio test
requests; it observes native initialization and management Read Info only.

Archive actual command/event pairs and any initialization failure. A returned
BD_ADDR is an observation, not proof of a provisioned public address. This
mode does not close board/address/RF, peer, E or production publication gates.
The regular USB driver remains observation-only. A new physical trial requires
its own identity/ownership checks and power-removal confirmation; never reuse
a consumed confirmation or report the empty VM preflight as hardware evidence.
